The best a compiler can do it appears is a jump table (indirect jump) The caveats being if the

[a] case values are large but contigous ( 999, 998, 997 ...)
    In which case the compiler should do a constant subtraction and use smaller index offsets 

[b] case values are sparse
    Well in this case, the best it can do is likely a Binary search followed by jump table after subtraction

[c] too many case statements
    https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=11823

    Well, in this case it will likely fall back to some sort of binary search + comparisons.

=====
Keep in mind, if certain switch cases are more commonly taken than others it might pay off to do a manual comparison
 if (value == 0) {
    handle_0();
 } else if (value == 1) {
   handle_1();
 } else {
   switch (value) {
      case 2: ..
      case 3: ...
   }	  
 }  

Reason being there is usually a threshold of branch comparisons which are faster than a single indirect jump (though if it is that 
common the ITP should help)

Anyways, these are all gray areas, watch out for the processor in question and optimize accordingly keeping these points in mind.

